WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:04.670
JOE: Abuse is when an individual is hurt
or treated badly, it is always wrong

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.330
and should never happen, but often does.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.520
It is never the fault of the
individual concerned it is

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:11.960
always the fault of the abuser.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:17.249
It can happen once or many times and can
include different types of abuse and may

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.390
get more serious the more it happens.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:23.120
BETH: Individuals with autism may
have problems protecting themselves

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.259
against harm, abuse or being taken
advantage of as they might not know

00:00:27.259 --> 00:00:31.410
what abuse is, or what has happened
to them and how it could affect them.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.179
They should be taught how to recognise
potential abuse and they should be able to

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:41.410
identify at least two trusted adults they
can report abuse to or go to for help.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.280
JOE: An individual should always
be listened to and believed when

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.250
they report abuse, they should also
be told about their rights and be

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:51.049
supported when reporting abuse.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:55.070
Anyone who works with or cares
for individuals with autism should

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.089
understand what abuse is, and work
to make sure it doesn’t happen.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.510
BETH: Families can educate themselves
and their loved one, on how to identify

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:07.149
warning signs and how to report
if abuse or neglect is suspected.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:11.030
They should talk openly to their
family member about personal safety,

00:01:11.289 --> 00:01:15.630
boundaries, saying ‘no’, and what
is a healthy and unhealthy touch.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.000
They may even try doing role
play to show what is a dangerous

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:20.840
situation and how to respond.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.869
JOE: The problem is that as a child
with autism grows up, they are taught to

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:29.789
respond more positively to people and to
be more responsive in social interactions.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:34.450
But when the potential is there for abuse,
it is harder for them to understand that

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:38.810
not all people are honest and trustworthy
and have their best interests at heart.

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:42.240
BETH: Individuals need to remember
that they do not have to do things

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.330
because others tell them to, and if
something feels wrong it probably is.

00:01:46.809 --> 00:01:50.190
If an individual has the confidence
to assert themselves and stand up

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:54.220
to a potential abuser, knowing that
when they say ‘no’ it means ‘no’,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.779
they are more likely to prevent
themselves from harm and abuse.

